## 2. Connecting to MotionAI

MotionAI runs an MCP server **inside the app** (Windows build). It is **off by default**.
Mohit turns it on once: home screen → **robot icon** (or project → **AI** tab → settings) →
**Claude control** → **Allow Claude to control MotionAI**. It stays on until switched off.

Server facts:

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Address | `http://127.0.0.1:7820` (port changeable under Claude control → Port) |
| MCP endpoint | `POST /mcp` — MCP "Streamable HTTP", JSON-RPC 2.0, JSON responses (SSE only when a `progressToken` is sent) |
| Health (no token) | `GET /health` → `{"ok":true,"app":"motionai","mcp":"2025-06-18"}` |
| Auth | every route except `/health` needs `Authorization: Bearer <token>` |
| Token | 256-bit random, shown and copyable under **Claude control**; refresh icon makes a new one (all clients must then use it). Also written for the stdio bridge to `%APPDATA%\MotionAI\mcp.json` and `%USERPROFILE%\.motionai\mcp.json` (removed when control is switched off) |
| Protocol versions | 2025-06-18 (newest), 2025-03-26, 2024-11-05 |
| Guards | loopback Host only; foreign `Origin` refused (except `POST /api/upload` from Flow origins) |
| REST mirror (debug) | `GET /api/tools`, `GET /api/state`, `GET /api/spec`, `POST /api/tools/<name>` with a JSON body of arguments |
| Upload (Flow → MotionAI) | `POST /api/upload?folder=<f>&name=<file.png>` with the bearer token, body = image bytes, from `https://labs.google` / `https://flow.google.com` pages → `Documents\MotionAI_Flow\<folder>\` |
| While Claude works | MotionAI shows an orange **Claude connected** pill; click it to see the action log |

### 2.1 Route A — Claude Desktop / Claude Code on the PC (stdio bridge)

`motionai-mcp.exe` (installed next to MotionAI.exe) is a stdio MCP bridge. It answers
`initialize`, `tools/list`, `resources/list` (and `get_manual`) offline, and forwards everything else
to the running app (it starts MotionAI through Explorer if it is closed and control is on).

- **Claude Desktop:** Claude control → **Add to Claude Desktop** (writes every config it finds:
  `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json` and every Microsoft Store package
  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Packages\Claude_<id>\LocalCache\Roaming\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json`), then
  **fully quit** Claude Desktop from the tray and reopen. By hand:
  ```json
  { "mcpServers": { "motionai": { "command": "C:\\Users\\<you>\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\MotionAI\\motionai-mcp.exe", "args": [] } } }
  ```
- **Claude Code:** `claude mcp add --scope user motionai -- "C:\Users\<you>\AppData\Local\Programs\MotionAI\motionai-mcp.exe"`
  or over HTTP: `claude mcp add --scope user --transport http motionai http://127.0.0.1:7820/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer <token>"`.
- Tools then appear as normal MCP tools (`motionai:get_state`, …). Progress notifications work.

### 2.2 Route B — a claude.ai / cloud session linked to the PC (built-in browser)

A cloud session cannot open sockets to the PC. It drives MotionAI through the **Claude desktop app's
built-in browser pane**, which runs on the PC and can reach `127.0.0.1`. This is how most Mohitflix
work is done.

1. Open the pane at the server (same origin, so `fetch('/mcp')` works and the Origin check passes):
   `Claude_Browser__preview_start {url: "http://127.0.0.1:7820/"}` (or `navigate`). The page shows a
   401 JSON error — that is fine; it is only the origin you need. If navigation fails, MotionAI is not
   running or control is off: ask Mohit to open MotionAI (and switch control on).
2. Get the token: ask Mohit to copy it from **Claude control** (or use the one from the last session —
   it only changes when he presses refresh). Never write it into files or commits.
3. Install helpers in the page with `Claude_Browser__javascript_tool` (tabId of that tab; other tabs
   such as a Flow tab may become active, so always pass the tabId):
   ```js
   window.T = '<token>';
   window.rpc = async (method, params = {}) => {
     const r = await fetch('/mcp', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Accept': 'application/json, text/event-stream', 'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + window.T },
       body: JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: Date.now(), method, params }) });
     return r.json();
   };
   window.call = async (name, args = {}) => {
     const j = await rpc('tools/call', { name, arguments: args });
     if (j.error) return 'ERR ' + JSON.stringify(j.error);
     return (j.result.content || []).map(c => c.type === 'text' ? c.text : '[' + c.type + ']').join('\n');
   };
   window.run = (commands) => call('run_commands', { commands });
   'ok'
   ```
   Then e.g. `await call('get_state')`, `await run([{op:'add_layer', …}])`.
4. **Globals vanish** when the page reloads or the pane restarts: if `call is not defined`, run step 3
   again. Long outputs: `slice()` strings (tool results over ~40k characters get cut by the browser tool).
5. **Seeing images in a cloud session.** Tool results with images come back as base64 in
   `content[].data`. The browser tool returns only text, so draw the image into the page and take a
   `Claude_Browser__computer {action:"screenshot"}` (or `zoom` for detail). The fastest way:
   ```js
   window.show = async (name, args) => {
     const j = await rpc('tools/call', { name, arguments: args });
     const img = (j.result.content || []).find(c => c.type === 'image');
     document.body.innerHTML = img ? `<img style="max-width:100%" src="data:${img.mimeType};base64,${img.data}">` : '<pre>no image</pre>';
     return (j.result.content || []).filter(c => c.type === 'text').map(c => c.text).join('\n').slice(0, 2000);
   };
   // await show('preview_shot', {from: 0, to: 8, count: 8, format: 'jpeg'}) → then screenshot the pane
   ```
   Use `format: "jpeg"` (≈5× smaller). The pane screenshot can be stale for a second: wait or re-take.
6. Per-call cost is one browser round trip, so use the **`batch`** tool (up to 50 calls in one
   request) and `export_status {wait_s}` instead of polling.

### 2.3 Route C — any HTTP MCP client on the PC

```json
{ "mcpServers": { "motionai": { "type": "http", "url": "http://127.0.0.1:7820/mcp",
  "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer <token>" } } } }
```
Debug with curl: `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" http://127.0.0.1:7820/api/state` and
`curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -X POST -d "{\"time\": 2}" http://127.0.0.1:7820/api/tools/seek`.

### 2.4 First calls in every new chat

1. `get_manual {"section":"toc"}` (this manual's table of contents) — or read the sections you need.
2. `get_state` — screen, open project, layers, media, running jobs, RAM.
3. `get_command_spec` once (the exact command reference of this build).
4. If no project is open: `list_projects` → `open_project {id}`, or `new_project {name, format}`.
5. Before building, check the build has what you plan to use: `list_effects`, `list_components`,
   `list_templates`, and `run_commands [{"op":"list_transitions"}]` (catalog in the log).

### 2.5 Connection troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause / fix |
|---|---|
| `navigation … denied or failed` / fetch fails | MotionAI not running, or Claude control off, or a different port. Ask Mohit to open MotionAI and switch control on |
| `401 Unauthorized: send "Authorization: Bearer <token>"` on `/` | Normal for the page itself. For `/mcp` it means a wrong/old token: get the current one from Claude control |
| `404 Not found. MCP endpoint: POST /mcp` | You posted to `/` — use `/mcp` |
| `403 Forbidden origin` | The request came from another site's page. Open the pane at `http://127.0.0.1:7820/` first |
| `ReferenceError: call is not defined` | Page reloaded; re-install the helpers (2.2 step 3) |
| `SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<'` | The JS ran in a different tab (e.g. the Flow tab became active). Pass the right `tabId` |
| Tool says control is off / app unavailable | Switch on *Allow Claude to control MotionAI* |
| `render_frame` says the window is minimised | Ask Mohit to restore the MotionAI window |
| Claude Desktop shows no `motionai` | Add to Claude Desktop again, fully quit Claude from the tray, check Settings → Developer |

## 3. The MCP tools (all 46, every argument)

Conventions: *(ro)* = read-only, never changes the project. *(edit)* = blocked while an export or
Director job runs. Optional arguments have defaults shown in brackets. Every tool returns MCP
`content` (text, JSON as text, or images) and `isError: true` with a reason on failure.

### 3.1 Orientation

**`get_manual`** *(ro)* — this manual. `section` (string, optional): `"toc"` (table of contents),
a chapter number (`"7"`, `"16"`), or words of a heading (`"camera"`, `"quirks"`, `"maps"`); `"spec"`
returns the live command reference (same as `get_command_spec`); `"all"` / omitted = the whole manual.
Answered even when no project is open (and by the stdio bridge while the app is closed).

**`get_state`** *(ro)* — snapshot: `app`, `screen` (home / editor / export…), `perf` (`ram_mb`,
`peak_ram_mb`, `errors_logged`), `project` (id, name, comp size / fps / duration, playhead, selection,
undo / redo availability, `editing_comp`, precomps, media ids, `shots`, every layer top-to-bottom with
id, type, start / end, transform values or keyframes, effects, masks) and running jobs. With no project
open it lists saved projects. **Call it first and after edits** to get ids.

**`get_command_spec`** *(ro)* — the full JSON edit-command reference of this build (~72 000 chars).
Ignore its "answer with one JSON object / reply" section: that is for the in-app assistant; you pass the
`commands` array to `run_commands`. Read it once per chat (or `get_manual {"section":"spec"}`).

**`list_effects`** *(ro)* — every effect: id, label, category, params (key, default, min, max, options).
**`list_components`** *(ro)* — every `add_component` type with its label.
**`list_templates`** *(ro)* — `category?`. Every insertable scene template: id, name, category,
duration, slots (key, kind text / media / medias / color, default).
Transitions catalog: `run_commands [{"op":"list_transitions"}]` (JSON in the log).

### 3.2 Editing

**`run_commands`** *(edit)* — `commands` (array of `{op, …}` objects, required). Runs them **in
order as ONE atomic batch = ONE undo step**, live in the editor. If any command fails nothing changes
and the error names the failing command (index + op). Returns created ids and a log. Give new layers
an `id` (and `start` / `duration`) so later commands in the same batch can address them. Use
`in_comp: "<comp id>"` on any command to run it inside a precomp.

**`get_layer`** *(ro)* — `layer` (required), `comp?` ("main" = main; default the open comp). Complete
JSON of one layer: keyframes with eases, effect params, masks, text style, shape, geo spec. The user's
view does not change.

**`get_layers`** *(ro)* — `comp?`, `full?` [false]. Every layer of any comp, top to bottom: id, name,
type, start, end, is3d, parent; `full:true` = complete layer JSON. No `open_comp` needed.

**`undo`** / **`redo`** *(edit)* — `steps?` [1] (1–50). Like Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y.

**`select`** — `layers?` (ids; `[]` deselects), `filter?` (layer filter as in `for_layers`, e.g.
`{"type":"text"}`), `mode?` replace | add | remove [replace], `camera?` (select the camera). Not an undo
step. Commands then accept `"layers": "selection"`.

**`seek`** — `time` (seconds, required). Moves the playhead. Commands without `time` / `start` use the
playhead, so **always pass explicit times** instead of relying on it.

**`play`** / **`pause`** — preview playback for Mohit to watch.

**`open_comp`** *(edit)* — `comp` (comp id from `get_state` "comps", or `"main"`). Editing, `get_state`
and rendering then use that composition. Prefer `in_comp` / `comp` arguments, which don't change
Mohit's view.

### 3.3 Projects

**`list_projects`** *(ro)* — saved projects, newest first: id, name, size, duration, layers, modified.
**`open_project`** *(edit)* — `id` (project id or exact name). Saves the current project first.
**`new_project`** *(edit)* — `name?` ["Project N"], `format?` youtube 1920×1080 | shorts 1080×1920 |
square 1080×1080 | portrait 1080×1350 | 4k 3840×2160 [youtube], `width?` / `height?` (override format),
`fps?` [30], `duration?` [10] (change later with `set_comp`), `template?` doc_intro | shorts_hook |
quote | title_3d. Creates, saves and opens it.
**`save_project`** — saves now (it also autosaves). **`rename_project`** — `name`.
**`close_project`** *(edit)* — saves and goes back to the home screen.

### 3.4 Media and files

**`import_media`** *(edit)* — `paths` (absolute paths on the PC, required), `add_to_timeline?` [false]
(also add each file as a layer, one after another), `start?` [playhead]. Copies photos / videos / audio
into the project and returns **media ids** for `add_layer {"media": …}`. Images larger than 4096 px on
the long side are downsized to 4096 on import (see 16). Paths must exist on Mohit's PC: Flow images in
`Documents\MotionAI_Flow\…`, Mohit's working folders (e.g. `C:\Users\1234\Downloads\NK_EP01\…`), or a
file you wrote with `write_file`.

**`list_media`** *(ro)* — media of the open project: id, type, name, pixel size, duration, file path,
AI description.

**`list_files`** *(ro)* — `path?`. Lists the user's `Documents\MotionAI*` folders (no path) or one
folder inside them (`"MotionAI_Flow/nk_ep01"`). Flow uploads land in `MotionAI_Flow`; your own files go
in `MotionAI_Files`.
**`read_file`** *(ro)* — `path` (starting with a MotionAI… folder, required), `encoding?` text | base64
[text], `max_bytes?` [2 000 000].
**`write_file`** — `path` (e.g. `"MotionAI_Files/plan.json"`, required), `text?` or `base64?`,
`append?`. Creates folders; returns the absolute path (feed it to `import_media`). Never writes outside
`Documents\MotionAI*`. (Mohit once refused Claude device access to `Documents\MotionAI_Files`; using the
app's own file tools for app work is fine, but don't use them to work around a refusal.)

### 3.5 Looking at the result

**`render_frame`** *(ro)* — `time?` [playhead], `width?` 160–1920 [960], `comp?`, `format?` png | jpeg
[png]. One frame rendered like the export (effects, 3D camera, text animation). Slow with particles /
light rays (~5–10 s). Prefer `preview_shot`.

**`preview_shot`** *(ro)* — `from?` [0], `to?` [comp end], `count?` 1–12 [6] (GIF: default 12, max 20),
`width?` tile width 160–960 [480] (GIF max 480), `comp?`, `format?` png | jpeg | gif [png]. Returns ONE
contact sheet (grid; each tile labelled `#n` and its time) plus the list of times. `gif` = small
animated GIF (≤ 20 frames, ≤ 2 s, ≤ 480 px; some clients show only the first frame). **The main way to
judge a move or a whole shot.**

**`check_scene`** *(ro)* — `comp?`, `from?` [0], `to?` [end]. Lint without rendering. Returns JSON
problems, each with layer id(s), time, severity and a `fix` list of ready `run_commands` commands:
black gaps; layers ending before their set / starting at the playhead; 3D moves uncovering empty
canvas edges; cut-outs floating above the ground; text overlapping, off-frame or outside the safe area;
known-broken effects (blur on a 3D layer, scramble on Devanagari); missing media. `fix_all` collects
every fix. **Review fixes before applying them.**

**`editor_screenshot`** *(ro)* — `width?` 160–1920 [960]. PNG of the live editor preview exactly as
Mohit sees it (the editor's draw path, not the export renderer). Use it to confirm what Mohit will see.

**`get_logs`** — `limit?` 1–300 [50], `level?` error | warning | info [info = all], `clear?`. Recent app
errors and events (Flutter errors, async errors, export / encoder failures), newest last.

### 3.6 Batching

**`batch`** — `calls` (1–50 `{name, arguments}`), `continue_on_error?` [false]. Runs several tool calls
in ONE request, in order, returning every result (images included), each headed `[#n tool] ok|ERROR`.
Stops at the first error unless `continue_on_error`. `batch` cannot contain `batch`. Standard loop:

```json
{"calls": [
  {"name": "run_commands", "arguments": {"commands": [ ... ]}},
  {"name": "check_scene",  "arguments": {"from": 0, "to": 8}},
  {"name": "preview_shot", "arguments": {"from": 0, "to": 8, "count": 8, "format": "jpeg"}}
]}
```

### 3.7 Export and jobs

**`export_video`** *(edit)* — `resolution?` 720 | 1080 | 1440 | 2160 (short side) [1080], `fps?`
[project fps], `folder?` (absolute) [last used; Windows default `Videos\MotionAI`], `file_name?`
["<project>_<time>.mp4"], `high_quality?` [true], `wait?` [false], `timeout?` (with wait: default 540 s
with a progressToken, else 50 s; max 540). Saves and exports the **main** composition to MP4 (Mohit
sees the export screen). Without `wait` returns a job id at once.

**`export_status`** *(ro)* — `job?` [latest], `wait_s?` 0–120 [0]. Phase setup / rendering /
finishing / done / failed, frame N of M, output file, error. `wait_s` holds the call until the export
finishes (or the time runs out): one call instead of polling.

**`job_status`** *(ro)* — `job?`. A background job (director, captions, export, depth):
running / done / failed / cancelled, message, result. Without `job`: all recent jobs.
**`cancel_job`** — `job` (required). Asks a running export or Director job to stop.

### 3.8 Automatic building

**`director_run`** *(edit)* — MotionAI's own Director (needs **MotionAI's own AI connection**, set in
the app's AI tab; without it build with `run_commands`). `brief` (required), `script?`, `narration?`
aroll | voiceover | phone_voice | silent, `aroll_media?`, `voice_media?`, `music_media?`, `language?`
hi | en | auto, `roman?` (Hinglish captions), `minutes?` 1–30 [3] (when the AI writes the script),
`captions?` [true], `sfx?` [true], `depth?` [true] (AI cut-outs), `review?` [true] (render and fix),
`look?` auto | cinematic_doc | shorts_punch | retro_film | noir | warm_travel | clean_minimal,
`wait?`, `timeout?`. Builds the whole timeline as one undo step. Returns a job id (minutes).

**`captions_generate`** *(edit)* — Whisper on the PC. `layer?` (video / audio layer; default selected,
else the first one), `style?` karaoke | word_pop | keyword | boxed | neon | punchy | typewriter | minimal
| hinglish | devanagari | decode [karaoke], `language?` hi | en | auto [hi], `roman?` (Hindi in Roman
script), `max_words?` 0–12 (0 = style default), `emphasis?` [true] (numbers, money, years, names),
`ai_emphasis?` (needs the app AI), `wait?`, `timeout?`. Adds caption layers with per-word timing.

**`suggest_patterns`** *(ro)* — `lines` (required: `[{"text":"…","start":0,"end":3.2}]`, times
optional, or one line per row), `style?` nr_doc, `tone?` auto | snow | desert | neutral, `presenter?`
[true], `asl?` [3.4], plus `presenter_min_gap?` [12], `presenter_max_gap?` [25], `cta_every?` [40],
`handle?` ["Mohitflix"] (see 13). Plans documentary patterns per beat; build with
`run_commands [{"op":"apply_patterns","plan":…,"assets":{…}}]`.

### 3.9 Templates and the asset library

**`apply_template`** *(edit)* — `id` (required), `slots?` (key → text | "#RRGGBB" | media id | "lib:<id>" |
list of ids), `start?` [playhead]. Inserts a scene template as one undo step (see 12.4).

**`library_search`** *(ro)* — `query?` (fuzzy; empty = newest), `role?` character | object | plate |
overlay | set_piece, `tags?`, `kit?`, `type?` image | video | audio, `transparent?`, `limit?` 1–100 [30].
Returns `lib:<id>` ids from Mohit's shared asset library.
**`library_insert`** *(edit)* — `id` ("lib:<id>", required), `add_to_timeline?` [true], `start?`,
`duration?`. Imports into the project (returns the media id) and optionally adds a layer.
**`library_import_folder`** *(edit)* — `path` (absolute folder, required), `recursive?` [true], `kit?`
[folder name], `tags?`, `green_screen?` auto | true | false [auto], `remove_mark?` [false]. Imports a
whole folder as one kit. Roles from file prefixes: `char_` character, `prop_` object, `plate_` / `bg_`
plate, `fx_` overlay, `cut_` set piece; a PNG wins over a same-named green-screen JPEG; duplicates are
reused. Slow for big folders.
**`library_clean`** *(edit)* — `ids?`, `kit?`, `all?`, `green_screen?` [auto], `remove_mark?` [true],
`restore?`. Keys flat green / blue screens to transparent PNG and / or inpaints the small AI sparkle
mark bottom-right (only when detected). Originals kept; `restore:true` undoes.
**`library_suggest`** *(ro)* — `brief` (required), `roles?`, `kit?`, `limit?` 1–30 [5] per role. Ranks
library assets for a shot.

### 3.10 Resources

- `motionai://manual` — this manual (text/markdown).
- `motionai://spec` — the command reference (text/plain), same as `get_command_spec`.
- `motionai://project` — the open project as complete JSON (all comps, layers, keyframes, media).

### 3.11 Long jobs and timeouts

`export_video`, `director_run`, `captions_generate` accept `wait:true`. With a client
`progressToken` (`params._meta.progressToken`) the server answers that request as `text/event-stream`
and sends `notifications/progress` (export: frame N of M) so the client timeout keeps resetting; it waits
up to `timeout` (default 540 s). Without a token it waits at most ~50 s (clients give up after about a
minute) and then returns the current status: follow up with `export_status {wait_s: 60}` /
`job_status`. In a browser-pane session there is no progress token: use `wait:false` + `export_status
{wait_s: 100}` in a loop.

## 4. The edit-command language

### 4.1 How a batch works

```json
{"commands": [
  {"op": "set_comp", "duration": 30, "background": "#000000"},
  {"op": "add_layer", "type": "image", "id": "bg", "media": "m_abc", "start": 0, "duration": 8, "fit": "cover"},
  {"op": "set_keyframe", "target": "bg", "prop": "scale", "time": 0, "value": [100, 100, 100], "ease": "easy_ease"},
  {"op": "set_keyframe", "target": "bg", "prop": "scale", "time": 8, "value": [112, 112, 100]}
]}
```

- Commands run **in order**; later commands can use ids created earlier in the same batch.
- Only reference ids that exist (from `get_state` / `get_layers`) or were created in the same batch.
- **Timing defaults (V2-B):** no `start` → 0 (NOT the playhead); no `start` and no `duration` → the
  whole comp (inside a shot / precomp: the whole shot). With only `start`, stills last 5 s.
  Video / audio / comp layers last their source. Pop-up components default to 5 s; "set" components
  (map, room, board, tv_frame, glass_screen, map_fly, route_follow, silhouette_crowd, poster, plaque,
  article) without start / duration span the whole comp. **Always give explicit `start` and
  `duration`** — it avoids every timing surprise.
- `layers[0]` is the **top** layer. `add_layer` inserts at the top unless `index` is given
  (`index: 0` = top; a larger index = lower). To put a new background under everything use a big index
  or `reorder_layer` afterwards.
- `in_comp: "<comp id>"` on ANY command runs it inside that comp.
- An unknown parameter on an effect is an error ("an effect only accepts its own params").

### 4.2 Values, properties, targets, eases

- Value = number or list `[x, y, z]`. Property paths: `position`, `anchor`, `scale` (percent),
  `rotation` (`[x, y, z]` degrees; 2D rotation is z), `opacity` (0–100), `volume` (0–200). One
  component: `"position.x"`, `"position.y"`, `"scale.x"`, `"rotation.z"` …
- `target` = a layer id or `"camera"`. Add `"effect": "<effect id>"` or `"mask": "<mask id>"` to address
  an effect's or mask's params (`prop` = the param key, e.g. `amount`).
- Colours `"#RRGGBB"`; transparent solids `"#00000000"`; many effect colours also accept `[r,g,b]`,
  and chroma accepts `"green"` / `"blue"`.
- Eases: `linear, hold, ease_in, ease_out, easy_ease, bounce, elastic, back`. **The ease on a keyframe
  shapes the motion FROM it to the next keyframe.**

### 4.3 Layers

**`add_layer`** `{type, id?, name?, media?, start?, duration?, source_in?, index?, is3d?, fit?, position?,
scale?, text?, font_size?, font_weight?, text_color?, align?, color?, width?, height?, animator?,
anim_duration?, box_color?, highlight_color?, shadow?, parent?, adjustment?, font?, text_style?, …}`

| type | needs | notes |
|---|---|---|
| `image` | `media` (image media id or `lib:<id>`) | `fit: contain | cover | none` (cover = fill the frame) |
| `video` | `media` | lasts its source; `source_in` = where in the clip it starts; `speed` via update_layer |
| `audio` | `media` | volume 0–200 (`set_keyframe prop volume` for fades) |
| `text` | `text` | font_size 90–160 for 1080p titles; `font`, `text_style`, `animator`, `box_color`, `shadow` |
| `solid` | `color` | `width` / `height` default = comp; `"#00000000"` transparent (for particles / adjustment) |
| `null` | — | invisible handle for parenting / grouping |
| `shape` | `kind` (+ size / points) | see 4.8 |
| `comp` | `comp` (comp id) | shows a precomp; `source_in`, `loop: true` |

- At creation only `position` and `scale` (and `is3d`, `parent`, timing, text / shape fields) are
  applied. **`anchor`, `rotation`, `opacity` and `blend` given to `add_layer` are ignored** — set them
  afterwards with `set_keyframe` at the layer start (anchor / rotation / opacity) and
  `update_layer {blend}` (see 16).
- `parent` must be an existing id. **Omit it when empty** — `"parent": ""` is an error.
- Adjustment layer: `{"op":"add_layer","type":"solid","adjustment":true,"color":"#00000000"}` — its
  effects apply to everything BELOW it (global grade, vignette, letterbox, grain, bloom, lens). Its
  opacity mixes the effect.

**`update_layer`** `{layer, name?, start? (moves keyframes too), duration?, source_in?, speed?, is3d?,
visible?, locked?, parent? (id or null), color?, text?, font_size?, font_weight?, text_color?, align?,
width?, height?, animator?, anim_duration?, box_color? ("none" removes), highlight_color?, shadow?,
outline?, extrude?, matte?, path?, blend?, font?, text_style?, stroke?, letter_spacing?, …}`.
**It does not change transform props:** `scale` / `position` / `rotation` / `opacity` on update_layer are
ignored — use `set_property` (static) or `set_keyframe`.

Other layer ops: `remove_layer {layer}` (children are un-parented, mattes cleared) ·
`duplicate_layer {layer, new_id?}` · `reorder_layer {layer, index}` · `trim_layer {layer, in?, out?}` ·
`split_layer {layer, time, new_id?}` · `replace_media {layer, media}` (keeps size / animation) ·
`swap_layer {layer, with, at, transition: pop|cut|fade|flash, duration 0.2, sfx}` (see 9.3).

### 4.4 Properties and keyframes

- `set_property {target, prop, value}` — static value; if the prop is animated it sets a keyframe at the
  playhead (so prefer `set_keyframe` with an explicit `time` on animated props).
- `set_keyframe {target, prop, time, value, ease?}` — adds / replaces a keyframe.
- `remove_keyframe {target, prop, time}` · `clear_keyframes {target, prop}` · `set_ease {target, prop,
  time, ease}`.
- `offset_property {layer, prop, by? | factor?}` — changes the value AND every keyframe (bulk nudge).
- Ken Burns: scale 100 → 112 over the clip with `easy_ease`; for documentary push-ins 100 → 106–110 in
  5–8 s is the reference speed.
- Anchor-point zoom (zoom into a point P without it moving): set `anchor` = P (in layer pixels) and
  `position` = P (in comp pixels) at the start keyframe, then animate `scale`.

### 4.5 Effects

`add_effect {layer, effect, id?, params?}` · `remove_effect {layer, effect}` ·
`set_effect_param {layer, effect, param, value, time?}` (with `time` it keyframes). Address the effect
by its id (default id = effect type). Animate params also with `set_keyframe {target, effect, prop:
<param>, time, value}`. The full list with every parameter, default and range is **Appendix A.1**
(57 effects). Highlights:

- Look: `color`, `tint`, `grade {lift, gain, saturation, contrast, amount}`, `mono`, `duotone`,
  `vignette`, `grain`, `light_leak`, `gradient`, `letterbox`, `halation`, `bloom`, `chromatic_aberration`,
  `lens_distortion`, `gate_weave`, `flicker`.
- Glow: `glow`, `deep_glow {amount 0-200, radius, threshold, spread, aspect %, chromatic, tint}`.
- Blur / focus: `blur` (not on 3D layers — use DOF), `edge_blur`, `motion_blur`, `radial_blur`.
- Atmosphere: `particles {kind confetti|leaves|stars|rain|snow|smoke|dust|bokeh|streaks|birds|clouds|
  debris|snow_spray|ice, amount, speed, color, direction}` (on a full-frame transparent solid),
  `dust`, `light_rays {angle, amount, color}`, `haze`.
- Distort: `curve {bend}`, `sway`, `glass_frame`, `torn_edge {side, amount}`, `page_curl`, `burn_edge`.
- Reveal / exit: `reveal {kind iris|ink|smoke|wipe, progress 0-100, softness, cx, cy}`,
  `disintegrate {progress, size, drift}`, `shatter`, `flash {amount (keyframe it), color}`, `glitch`.
- Keying: `chroma_key {color "green"|"blue"|"#RRGGBB", tolerance 50, softness 20, spill 50, choke
  -50..50, contrast, view "final"|"matte"}`.
- Compositing realism: `contact_shadow`, `light_wrap`, `shadow {distance, angle, blur, opacity}`,
  `outline {width, color}`, `silhouette {color, glow}`.

Looks in one command: `apply_style {style, layers?}` with `cinematic_doc, shorts_punch, retro_film, noir,
warm_travel, clean_minimal` (image / video layers) — or put a doc look on an **adjustment layer**:
`doc_golden_hour, doc_cold_night, doc_archive_sepia, doc_teal_orange, doc_noir_contrast,
doc_desaturated_war`, plus `film_look` (grain + gate weave + halation + vignette + flicker; combine with a
doc_* look).

### 4.6 Masks, track mattes, blend modes, motion paths

- `add_mask {layer, shape: ellipse|rect, center? [x,y] (layer px), size? [w,h], feather?, invert?, id?}` ·
  `update_mask {layer, mask, shape?, invert?}` · `remove_mask {layer, mask}`. Animate `center` / `size` /
  `feather` with `set_keyframe {target, mask, prop, time, value}`. Masks also clip what effects draw past
  the layer edge.
- Track matte: `update_layer {layer, matte: {layer: <matte id>, mode: alpha|inverse}}` — show the layer
  only inside / outside the matte layer (the matte is hidden). A keyed layer can be a matte.
- Blend: `update_layer {layer, blend: normal|screen|add|lighten|multiply|overlay|darken|soft_light|
  hard_light|color_dodge|difference}` — real blending over everything below. Not together with a track
  matte on the same layer. Double exposure = `screen` a photo over footage; highlighter / ink on paper =
  `multiply`.
- Motion path: `update_layer {layer, path: {points: [[x,y], …], smooth: true, orient: false}}` or
  `{from:[x,y], to:[x,y], bend: px, orient}`, then keyframe `path_progress` 0 → 100. Points are in the
  parent's space (comp px without a parent); while a path is set, `position` x/y is an offset from it.
  `path: "none"` removes it. Works on nulls too (children follow).

### 4.7 Composition, playback-wide settings, animate in / out

- `set_comp {name?, width?, height?, fps?, duration?, background?, motion_blur?: bool, dof?: {focus,
  aperture}}`. Make the duration fit the content.
- `animate_in` / `animate_out {layer, preset, duration?}` — presets: `fade, zoom_in, zoom_out, slide_left,
  slide_right, slide_up, slide_down, blur, spin, pop, whip, shatter, iris, ink, smoke, flash, glitch_flash,
  punch_in, swing_in {rotation 20, scale_from 2.5, background: layer id}, exposure_in {from 3.0}`.
  For scene changes prefer the `transition` op (9.1).

### 4.8 Shapes

`add_layer {type: "shape", kind: rect|ellipse|polygon|star|line|path, width, height, fill, fill2 (gradient),
stroke, stroke_width, corner, sides, inner, dash: [on, off], arrow_end, arrow_start, points: [[x,y],…]
(comp px, for line / path), smooth, closed}` (`shape` is accepted as an alias of `kind`).
Animate drawing with `set_keyframe prop "trim" [start%, end%]`, e.g. `[0,0]` → `[0,100]` (a pen
circle: ellipse, no fill, red stroke 6, trim 0 → 100 in 0.35 s). Highlighter bar: rect, fill
`#F5DE28`, opacity 60, blend multiply, scale.x 0 → 100 with the anchor at the left edge.

### 4.9 Text

Fields on `add_layer` / `update_layer` text layers:

- `text`, `font_size`, `font_weight` (100–900), `text_color`, `align` (left | center | right),
  `box_color` (background box; `"none"` removes), `highlight_color`, `shadow`, `outline` (outline-only
  stroke width), `stroke` (px edge behind FILLED letters, or `{width, color}`; `"none"` removes),
  `stroke_color`, `italic`, `letter_spacing`, `extrude` (depth px or `{depth, color}` = thick 3D edge;
  0 removes), `lead_color` + `lead_chars` (typewriter: newest letters coloured), `word_times` [s after the
  layer start, one per word] + `dim_color` (word_fade_grey).
- **Fonts** (`font: "<family>"`, unknown names rejected): condensed Anton, Bebas Neue, Oswald, Teko,
  Barlow Condensed · serif Playfair Display, DM Serif Display, Cinzel · script Great Vibes, Dancing
  Script · display Permanent Marker · mono / typewriter Special Elite, Roboto Mono, Share Tech Mono ·
  sans Inter, Montserrat, Poppins (incl. Poppins 800) · comic Comic Neue · Hindi (Devanagari + Latin)
  Noto Sans Devanagari, Hind, Mukta, Baloo 2, Rozha One, Yatra One, Kalam (Teko and Poppins cover
  Devanagari too). Devanagari in a Latin-only font automatically uses a matching Hindi font
  (Anton→Teko, serif→Rozha One, script→Kalam, sans→Mukta).
- **Text styles** (`text_style: "<preset>"`, explicit keys override): `doc_title` (Anton + 3D extrude),
  `gold_word`, `date_serif` (Playfair 900 years), `script_name`, `neon_outline`, `typewriter` (Special
  Elite typing on), `headline_red` (Oswald on a red box), `roman_caps` (Cinzel gold), `marker_note`,
  `caption_bold`, `hindi_bold`, `hindi_title`, `hindi_handwritten`, `caption_red_outline`,
  `stat_red_outline`, `osd_timestamp` (VCR stamp), `label_yellow_outline`, `stat_yellow`.
- **Animators** (`animator`, with `anim_duration` = whole animation): `none, typewriter, word_pop,
  fade_up, slide_up, karaoke, random_pop, scramble, write_on (handwriting wipe per word),
  typewriter_soft, letter_drop, word_fade_grey`. `scramble` is broken on Devanagari (check_scene flags
  it).
- **Marks** on text: `marks: [{phrase, style: highlight|circle|underline|color, delay (s after layer
  start), duration, color}]`. **Counters:** `counter: {from, to, prefix, suffix, decimals, delay,
  duration}`.
- Typing sound synced per letter: `add_sfx {kind: "typewriter", layer: <text layer with animator
  typewriter>}`.

## 5. Camera, 3D, depth and shots

### 5.1 Camera modes and simple moves

- `set_camera_mode {mode: off|2d|3d}`.
- `camera_move {preset, start?, duration?, amount?, ease?, direction?: left|right|up|down}` — each move
  starts from the camera state at `start`.
  - 3D presets: `push_in, pull_out, orbit (amount = degrees), dolly_zoom, drone_reveal, truck, pedestal`.
  - 2D presets: `pan_2d, zoom_in_2d, zoom_out_2d, rotate_2d, shake (amount = px)`.
  - `punch_in {point? [x,y] | target?, amount? (×2.6), duration? (0.45), ease? (ease_in), layer? | map?,
    center?: true, blur?: true, sound?: true | "whoosh" | false}` — fast zoom punch with motion blur and
    whoosh (emphasis / right before a twist). With a 3D camera pass `layer`/`map` to punch a group.
- Keyframe the camera directly: `set_keyframe {target: "camera", prop: "position" | "point_of_interest"
  | "rotation" | "zoom", time, value}` (check `get_state` → camera for the exact prop names of this
  build).
- `camera_shake {start, duration?, amount? 0-100 (40), style: handheld|walk|impact|earthquake}`.

### 5.2 After-Effects camera (win-40)

`set_camera {type?: one_node | two_node, lens_mm?: 15|20|24|28|35|50|80|135|200 (any 5..1000),
focus_lock?: true, blur_level?: % (DOF strength), aperture?, focus?, orientation? [pitch, yaw, roll],
time? (keyframe these at a time)}`.

- `one_node` aims with `orientation`; `two_node` looks at its point of interest (orientation is applied on top,
  like AE). Switching between them keeps the current aim.
- win-43: camera `rotation` [x tilt (+ = look down), y pan (+ = turn right), z roll]° — AE's X/Y/Z Rotation, on top
  of the aim, for both types: `set_property {target: "camera", prop: "rotation", value: [x, y, z]}` (or
  `"rotation.y"`); keyframe it like any property. The editor shows it with Position, Point of Interest,
  Orientation, Zoom / focal length / angle of view and Depth of Field as AE-style scrub fields; Mohit can also
  drag on-screen gizmos (W move, E rotate, R scale, C camera orbit / pan / dolly, X world/local) in every view.
- `focus_lock: true` keeps the focus distance on the point of interest (AE "Lock to Zoom" style).
- `set_view_layout {layout: "1"|"2"|"4"|"1+3", views?: [active|front|left|top|back|right|bottom|custom1|
  custom2|custom3], custom?: {custom1: [yaw, pitch, distance]}}` — editor viewer panes. Working views
  show 3D layers, the camera frustum and the point of interest. This only changes Mohit's editor
  panes, not the render.

### 5.3 Depth of field and rack focus

- `set_dof {focus: "target" (follows the camera_shot target) | layer id | px | "off", aperture?
  (20 default; 10 subtle, 40 strong)}` or `set_comp {dof: {focus, aperture}}`.
- Rack focus: `set_dof {keys: [{t: 3.0, focus: "fg_layer"}, {t: 4.2, focus: "wall_layer"}],
  aperture: 30}` — keyframed focus between subjects (3D layers).
- 2D fallback rack focus: keyframe `blur` amount on the layers (0 ↔ 12) in opposite directions.
  `blur` on a 3D layer is a known-broken combo (check_scene flags it): use DOF for 3D layers.

### 5.4 "Compose flat, play deep": make_3d + camera_shot

1. Lay the shot out flat (2D): plate at the back, cut-outs in front, foreground objects on top.
2. `make_3d {layers?: [ids] (default: everything visible at start), bands?: {id: sky|far|mid|near|front},
   start?, haze? 0-100 (25; 0 = off), contact_shadows?: true}` — puts each layer on a depth band (guessed
   from name / type / size / position if not given), converts to 3D, keeps the first frame identical.
   Parented layers move with their group root. Far / sky layers get atmospheric haze; near / mid cut-outs
   standing in the lower frame get a contact shadow.
3. `camera_shot {move, target?: layer id, point?: [x,y], start, duration, ease?, intensity? 0-1,
   direction?: left|right, cut_to?: layer id}`.

| move | what it does |
|---|---|
| `drift` | slow push-in + drift (the default documentary feel) |
| `fly_to` | flies toward a target layer / point |
| `push_through` | flies through the target (door / window); turns motion blur on |
| `pass_by` | camera passes by sideways (direction) |
| `reveal` | starts behind the nearest front layer, reveals the scene |
| `pull_back` | starts in extreme close-up, pulls back |
| `orbit` | orbits ≤ 25° (direction) |
| `truck` | real sideways dolly (camera and aim slide together: strongest parallax) |
| `arc` | orbit up to 40° with a slight push-in |
| `crane` | rises while tilting to the target (direction down = sinks) |

The camera always stays ≥ 30 px from every layer and full-frame backgrounds grow so no edge shows.
`cut_to` cuts to that shot at the end behind a white flash (the 3D set ends there) — avoid for
Mohitflix (prefer continuous moves).

- Reuse: `save_shot {name, from?, to?}` / `load_shot {name, start, layers?: {savedId: newId}}`;
  `save_recipe {name, layer}` / `apply_recipe {name, layer, start?}` / `delete_recipe {name}` (a layer's
  move + transition + atmosphere + look + frame, keyframes relative to its start).

### 5.5 Manual 2.5D parallax rig (Mohit's proven method)

When `make_3d` framing is not what you want, build parallax by hand with null rigs per depth (this is
what the NK cold open used): per comp `C`, a back rig `C_rgB` (k = 0.55) and a front rig `C_rgF`
(k = 1.4). For a camera scale move s(t) around centre C and drift D:

- rig scale `s_k = 1 + (s − 1) · k`
- rig position `pos = C − (P − C) · (s_k − 1) + D · k · t / d` (P = push point the camera moves toward,
  D = ±70 px drift, d = shot length) — this keeps P fixed on screen while the rig scales
- background plate parented to `C_rgB` (scale it to ~126 % so edges never show); foreground pieces
  (table, radio, door edge, lamp) parented to `C_rgF`; mid layers unparented (k = 1).
- Set the rig's anchor / rotation by `set_keyframe` at t0 (not at `add_layer`).

### 5.6 Shots and sequences (one command = one parallax shot)

`add_shot {id?, name?, start? (default: end of the last shot in main), duration? (6), style?, plate: media
id, layers: [{media, role?: sky|far|mid|near|front|ground, id?, name?, x? (0..1 centre), ground? (0..1
where its BOTTOM stands), height? (0..1 of frame height), flip?, hero?: true, effects?: ["sway" |
{effect, params}]}], floor? (0.55–1.2, default 0.88), ground?: "auto" | "shadows" | "none" | floor media,
ground_color?, camera?: [{move, target?: layer | media | role | "hero", start, duration, intensity, ease?,
direction?}] | "none", atmosphere?: "dust" | ["dust","haze"] | {dust: 40, haze: 30} (dust, rain, snow,
smoke, fog, haze, light_rays, birds, leaves, bokeh, embers, clouds, stars), look?, title?: "text" |
{text, subtitle?, font?, text_style?, at?, position?}, caption?: "text" | {text, at?, duration?,
position?, font?}, keep_far_visible? (true)}`

- Builds a precomp (own 3D camera) with the plate + cut-outs auto-sized by role (front ≤ 45 % of frame
  height, people at mid ≈ 62 %, far ≈ 27 %), feet on one floor so nothing floats, depth bands, camera
  moves that never touch a layer or show an empty edge; then ONE comp layer in main. Shot id = precomp
  id = comp layer id (`in_comp: <shot id>` edits inside).
- Styles: `opening_reveal` (reveal from behind the foreground + drift, haze, cinematic_doc),
  `character_focus` (drift → fly_to hero), `crowd_pass` (pass_by, warm_travel), `slow_push_detail` (one
  slow fly_to, light rays), `crash_pull_back` (tight → crash back to wide, noir).
- `sequence {shots: [add_shot specs], start?, transition?: type, transition_duration?, transitions?:
  [{between: [i, i+1], type, duration? (0.6), overlap?: true, …params}]}` — shots back to back; a
  transition overlaps the next shot ("cut" = none).
- `update_shot {shot, …fields, ripple?}` rebuilds with changed fields (refuses if the shot comp is used
  elsewhere) · `remove_shot {shot, ripple?: true}`. `get_state` → `shots` lists them.
- Known: `add_shot` frames **exteriors** well; **interiors** via add_shot / make_3d + camera_shot were
  framed badly in tests — for rooms prefer the `room` component (5.7) or the manual rig (5.5).

### 5.7 Real 3D sets: the `room` component

`add_component {type: "room", back?|wall?, left?, right?, floor?, ceiling? (media ids, one image per plane),
items?: [media id | {media, x (0..1 across the floor, or px), z (0 front .. 1 back), height px, role:
person|prop, shadow}], foreground?: [{media, x 0..1, z -0.9..0 (in front of the room), height, y}],
width?, depth?, height? (px), color?, shadows? (true)}` — walls / floor / ceiling are 3D planes, so camera
moves show true perspective; people stand ON the floor at their depth with contact shadows; foreground
objects near the lens give depth and natural wipes. **Prefer it to one flat plate whenever the camera
moves sideways.** Template `set_room` (3D sets) builds one from slots back / left / right / floor /
people / foreground. Street equivalent: template `set_street`.

`board {media, curve 0-100 (or bend), frame: wood|glass, pop: true|false, title?}` — curved board, the
picture pops out with overshoot.

### 5.8 Photo → 3D (exists; not the Mohitflix method)

`photo_to_3d {media | layer, layers?: 3-6 (4), start?, duration?, camera?: move | {move, intensity,
direction, start, duration} | "none", intensity? 0-1 (0.5), model?: auto|depth|fake}` — estimates depth
(AI model on Windows), cuts the photo into depth layers, fills behind, adds the move. Background job
(`depth_N`, poll `job_status`). `depth_map {media, model?}` → a grayscale depth image. **Mohit's rule: no
fake depth maps — build layered scenes from separate images instead.** Use only if he asks.

### 5.9 Realism: make cut-outs look photographed

- `match_colour {layer: cut-out, to?: plate | "below", amount? 0-100 (60; exposure capped 75–140 %),
  light_wrap?: true}` — run for every pasted person / prop.
- Effects: `contact_shadow {opacity 55, softness, length, angle (light direction: 0 right, 90 overhead,
  180 left), color, cast 0-100}`, `light_wrap {amount, width, color}`, `grade`, `haze`, `bloom`,
  `halation`, `chromatic_aberration`, `lens_distortion`, `gate_weave`.
- Recipe: make_3d → match_colour on each cut-out → camera_shot → set_dof {focus:"target"} → a doc_* look
  (+ film_look) on an adjustment layer → camera_shake handheld (amount 15–25) for a filmed feel.

### 5.10 Classic parallax helper

`parallax_rig {layers: [backId … frontId], depth? (px between layers, 500), push? (350), start?,
duration?}` — quick 3D parallax for a stack of layers.

