## 12. Assets: import, library, templates, Google Flow

### 12.1 Import rules

- `import_media {paths}` → media ids. Keep images ≤ 4096 px on the long side (bigger is downsized) and
  scale up with keyframes instead. 2752×1536 (2× a Flow 1376×768) is a good plate size.
- Transparent cut-outs: PNG with alpha. Green-screen images: key with `chroma_key` (or
  `library_clean {green_screen: "true"}` / `library_import_folder {green_screen: "auto"}`).
- Crop / inpaint the small Flow "sparkle" / "AI" mark bottom-right before use (`library_clean
  {remove_mark: true}` or do it before import).
- Tall pages (e.g. 1600×2000 article images) did not render in tests; landscape 1920×1300 works.

### 12.2 The shared asset library

Library ids `lib:<id>` work anywhere a media id does (`add_layer`, `apply_template` slots, Director).
`library_import_folder` (roles from prefixes `char_ prop_ plate_/bg_ fx_ cut_`), `library_search`,
`library_suggest {brief}`, `library_insert`, `library_clean`. Use plates as the back layer, characters /
objects / set pieces (transparent cut-outs) above, overlays on top, then make_3d / rigs.

### 12.3 Google Flow → MotionAI

0. **Fastest: the Flow panel** (win-43, Windows): Google Flow inside MotionAI (WebView2, its own sign-in
   that persists; home screen ✦ button). `flow_panel_open {url?}` opens it (a Flow project url opens that
   project; `show:false` keeps it off screen); `flow_pull {folder, match?, limit?}` takes **every finished
   image** of the open project straight into the library in seconds: the signed-in page fetches the
   originals 4 at a time and hands them to the app over WebView2 (no browser download, no ZIP, no
   localhost request), saved to `Documents\MotionAI_Flow\<folder>\` and imported as one kit. Names come
   from Flow titles with a role prefix: a title already starting `char_`/`prop_`/`plate_`/`fx_` keeps it;
   otherwise a green-screen border → `char_` (person words) or `prop_`, a black border → `fx_`, else
   `plate_`. `flow_panel_eval {code}` runs page JavaScript (read titles, rename tiles); `flow_panel_close`
   hides it. Generate in Flow (flow-mcp's `flow_agent_images`, or by hand in the panel), then `flow_pull`.
   First use: the user signs in to Google once in the panel.
1. **Best for generating: Mohit's flow-mcp** (`C:\Users\1234\flow-mcp`, "Flow Studio"): `flow_agent_images {project,
   scenes: [≤ 50 prompts], aspect_ratio, references?, scene_references?}` hands the whole batch to Flow's
   own Agent mode (parallel, 0 credits, one ZIP back into `~/flow-mcp-out/<project>/`). It must be
   running (Claude Desktop config entry `flow`, or double-click `Flow Studio.cmd`; local API
   `127.0.0.1:8787` with the token in `~/.flow-mcp/token`). Lessons from its notes: green-screen cut-outs
   go **without** reference images (references contaminate backgrounds / props); don't share one long
   style paragraph across scenes (it breaks matching); stills cost 0 credits.
2. **Built-in browser + `tool/flow_helper.js`** (win-30): paste into a signed-in Flow tab;
   `flowSetup({token, folder})`, `flowQueue([{id, prompt}])`, `flowNext()` + a real Enter key press (Flow
   ignores synthetic clicks), `await flowCollect()` → images go straight to `POST
   http://127.0.0.1:7820/api/upload` → `Documents\MotionAI_Flow\<folder>\` (several per prompt kept as
   id_1, id_2 …). Then `list_files` / `import_media`.
3. Manual Flow settings that worked: Image mode, 16:9, Nano Banana 2.1, Thinking High, ×1–×4 (0
   credits). Make the wide plate first, then add it as an **ingredient** and start close-ups with "Same
   room as the reference image." ~30 s between prompts if driving by hand.
4. **Stop on any CAPTCHA / unusual-activity warning** (rule 0.1.9).

Prompting for layered scenes: plates **without people**; people / props as **full-body cut-outs on a flat
chroma-key green background, evenly lit, no shadow on the background, sharp edges**; foreground
elements (door edge, lamp, curtain) on green; photoreal 35–85 mm language; "no text, no logos".

### 12.4 Scene templates

`list_templates` → `apply_template {id, slots, start}`. Families (Appendix A.4 lists them):
- Built-ins: intros (viral_3d_intro, years_ago_tiles, date_title_intro), showcases (glass_montage,
  article_reveal, silhouette_reveal, scanner, icon_flow, year_ruler, day_counter, passenger_tally),
  transitions (flash_hit, light_leak, foreground_wipe, ink_reveal), captions (glow_word,
  word_pop_caption, scramble_title, stat_title), lower thirds (date_stamp), endings (subscribe_end,
  memorial, social_bug), maps (country_focus, map_journey, map_fly), 3D (village_flythrough,
  photo_parallax).
- 3D sets (each builds its own precomp, every slot optional, `look` slot warm | cold | noir | vintage):
  set_photo_pile_title, set_pieces_on_board, set_stage_lineup, set_topics_to_idea,
  set_pinned_portraits, set_room, set_street, set_push_through, set_character_intro, set_article_desk.
- `doc_tr_<type>` — one demo per transition (slots a, b media).
- `pattern_<id>` — one per story pattern (13.2).
- User templates: right-click a layer → Save as template (category "My templates").

